INT. FOREVER FRIENDS PET MEMORIAL PARLOR - VIEWING ROOM - DAY

Soft lilac walls. A small satin-lined coffin on a stand. Lilies. A framed photo of a hamster named Pickle.

GWENDOLYN POPPLEWELL, 40s, black veil and a pinched face, dabs a handkerchief at dry eyes. RAYMOND TSOSIE-KANE, 30s, in a tie too tight for him, adjusts a floral arrangement without being asked.

RAYMOND
(to the empty chairs)
Friends. Family. We gather today to honor Pickle.

GWENDOLYN
(whispering)
Raymond. Nobody's here.

RAYMOND
(to her)
Practice, Mrs. Popplewell.

GWENDOLYN
I'm Gwendolyn.

RAYMOND
Gwendolyn. Forgive me.

She sniffs. The room is calm. A bell chimes from the hall.

GWENDOLYN
He was a good hamster.

RAYMOND
The very best.

GWENDOLYN
Did you know him?

RAYMOND
Only from the photograph.

GWENDOLYN
(to herself)
Seven hundred dollars for a hamster.

RAYMOND
Mm?

GWENDOLYN
Nothing.

Raymond lifts the coffin lid to adjust the satin. He stops. Peers in. Gently lowers the lid and clears his throat.

RAYMOND
Mrs. Popplewell. Gwendolyn. How much did you say Pickle weighed?

GWENDOLYN
Two ounces.

RAYMOND
Right.

GWENDOLYN
Why?

Raymond turns the little coffin around. Opens it again. Inside, on the satin, lies a perfectly preserved dinner roll.

RAYMOND
(to the room)
Folks, I think we have a clerical error.

Gwendolyn lowers her handkerchief. Stares at the roll.

GWENDOLYN
Oh, thank God.

RAYMOND
Pardon?

GWENDOLYN
(brightly)
Pickle's in the car.
